      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — November 5, 2026</h1>

      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Thursday, November 5, 2026)</p>

      <p class="top-story"><strong>Top Story:</strong> The morning after Texas elected a governor who wants a data center moratorium, Riot Platforms signed a 15-year, 910 MW lease with an unnamed frontier AI lab at its Corsicana site, worth about $28 billion. Its CEO told analysts the site's power is already approved and needs no new state sign-off.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📰 Today's News</h2>

      <h3>1. 🏗️ Riot Signs a $28 Billion, 910 MW AI Lease at Corsicana the Morning After the Election</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Riot Platforms reported third-quarter results Wednesday, Nov. 4, before an 8 a.m. Eastern call, and announced a 15-year triple-net powered land lease covering its entire 332-acre Corsicana campus in Navarro County. The tenant is described only as "a leading Frontier AI Lab." It's the same lab that already leases space at Riot's Rockdale site.</li>
        <li>The lease covers 910 MW of critical IT capacity in three phases: 400 MW in September 2027, 200 MW in January 2028 and 310 MW in July 2028. Riot puts the contracted rent at about $28 billion over the initial term, or about $1.8 billion a year on average, and about $54 billion if both five-year extensions are used.</li>
        <li>Riot supplies the land, power infrastructure and powered pads. The tenant owns and runs the computers and pays substantially all operating costs. Riot estimates about $6 million per IT megawatt, about $5.5 billion in all. It funds $2 million per MW itself and reimburses the rest in phases, which it expects to finance with non-recourse project debt. CEO Jason Les called it "the largest lease in Riot's history."</li>
        <li>On the call, an analyst asked about "the uncertainty around the elections last night." Les said both Corsicana and Rockdale already have approved interconnections and power: "There's not additional approval needed to execute on developments here." He said Corsicana already has a substation, and an expansion of it is underway (Investing.com transcript).</li>
        <li>Riot shares rose 12.7% in premarket trading, to $15.80. KBW still put its Outperform rating and $20 target under review. Analyst Stephen Glagola called Corsicana "the best-positioned Texas site in our coverage," but said "the election adds uncertainty around timing" (Blockspace).</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Riot's answer to the election is that the hard part is already done: its power is approved, so a pause on new approvals doesn't touch it. That's the line between Texas projects that keep moving and projects that wait. The moratorium Hinojosa is promising would hit projects still waiting on a permit or a grid study, not those that already have a substation.</p>

      <h3>2. 🗳️ Democrats Also Win the Texas House and the Comptroller's Office, and Hinojosa Will Pick the Next TCEQ and PUC Commissioners</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>The Texas Tribune reported Wednesday that Hinojosa takes office Jan. 19 and will work with a Democratic-controlled Texas House. MIT Technology Review said it's the first time in 24 years that Democrats will run the chamber. Republicans kept the Senate, as reported Wednesday.</li>
        <li>Democratic state Sen. Sarah Eckhardt of Austin defeated Comptroller Don Huffines, whom Abbott appointed three months ago, the Tribune reported late Tuesday. She'll be the first Democratic comptroller since 1999. The comptroller's office administers the data center sales tax exemption and distributes sales tax revenue to local governments. (Wednesday's edition, citing early returns, said Huffines led. He lost.)</li>
        <li>As governor, Hinojosa will appoint commissioners to TCEQ and the Public Utility Commission. Abbott appointed every current member, Inside Climate News noted. Public Citizen's Adrian Shelley said "the new state leadership has a clear mandate to rein in the data center industry."</li>
        <li>Abbott's pause still stands. As of Thursday morning he had not lifted it, Data Center Dynamics reported. Before the election Hinojosa had predicted he would, calling a temporary moratorium "a scam because Abbott will likely lift it on November 4th." Her "Texans First Data Center Priorities" would require union jobs, ban nondisclosure agreements, make companies prove household bills won't rise, end subsidies, void water contracts that threaten a community's supply, and give local communities a vote on projects.</li>
        <li>Industry reaction was cautious. Jess Heck of the Data Center Coalition told Reuters that data centers "can continue to play a crucial role" in Texas's economy. Austin McCarty, a K&amp;L Gates government affairs adviser, said some projects will be delayed or leave the state while others rush to finish before new rules take hold: "It's a brand-new day in Texas for a ton of what was probably considered settled policy."</li>
        <li>In North Texas, UT Austin's Michael Webber told NBC 5 that "a moratorium or the threat of a moratorium is enough to get the developers to consider other states." He still expects Texas to become the top U.S. market, with Dallas-Fort Worth at its center. Public Citizen's Kathryn Guerra put it more simply to the Tribune: "We've just seen real anger in communities."</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Wednesday's picture was a Democratic governor up against a Republican Legislature. Thursday's is different: Democrats hold the House, the comptroller's office, and the power to appoint the regulators who issue permits and run grid rules. A moratorium still needs the Senate, but much of what Hinojosa wants, from slower TCEQ permits to a tougher PUC, can happen through appointments.</p>

      <h3>3. 📊 ERCOT's First Look at the Data Center Survey: 433 Projects, 177 GW, and 45 GW of It in North Texas</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>In a letter filed at 6 p.m. Wednesday in PUC Project 59142, ERCOT General Counsel Chad Seely sent commissioners three presentations for today's open meeting. The first summarizes the Data Center State and Community Impact request for information. ERCOT sent it to 465 loads, and 433, or 93%, responded, covering about 177 GW, or 97% of requested interconnection capacity. All 245 Large Loads (75 MW or more) responded. Thirty-two Medium Load responses are still missing.</li>
        <li>Large Loads account for about 166 GW, or 94% of the capacity. Thirty-three projects requesting more than 1 GW each make up 36% of the total. By load zone, West Texas has the most capacity at 73 GW. North Texas, the zone that includes Dallas-Fort Worth, has 69 Large Loads, 85 Medium Loads and 45 GW. South has 49 GW and Houston 10 GW.</li>
        <li>The biggest gap in the answers is cooling. Large Loads marked 37% of cooling questions "N/A or unable to answer," and many said they hadn't chosen a cooling design yet. Water questions went unanswered 21% of the time for Large Loads and 29% for Medium Loads. Projects planning to energize in 2028 or later answered N/A about 30% more often. About 95% of facilities, representing 175.9 GW, said they won't mine cryptocurrency. ERCOT calls all of this "preliminary" and is targeting a final public report on Dec. 10.</li>
        <li>A second presentation covers Batch Zero eligibility checks. ERCOT and outside counsel have reviewed answers to about 5,140 questions from 257 developers, and ERCOT expects to send follow-up questions to 80% of them, each with a five-business-day deadline. Problems that keep coming up include no proof of site control, missing affiliate documentation, and no evidence that long-lead equipment is actually on order.</li>
        <li>ERCOT also sent 132 utilities an RFI on Medium Loads (10 MW to under 75 MW), with answers due 5 p.m. Friday, Nov. 6. It cited a "significant increase" in such requests over the summer, and warned that capacity going to Medium Loads "could be the same capacity allocated to a Base Load in Batch Zero."</li>
        <li>Separately, Skybox Datacenters asked commissioners in a letter filed at noon Wednesday to add its complaint (Docket 60431) to today's agenda. Skybox said it "appears to have been inadvertently omitted" when the other four Batch Zero complaints were scheduled. ERCOT's letter said it will file its own good-cause exception next week for loads that claimed to be energized but weren't by July 10, so the PUC can take it up Dec. 17.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> For the first time, there's a statewide count with numbers attached, and North Texas alone has asked for 45 GW. The cooling and water gaps matter more than the totals. Whoever writes the new rules will see that a large share of the projects in line still can't say how they'll cool their buildings or how much water they'll use.</p>

      <h3>4. 📉 Analysts Cut Their Odds on Texas Leases and Their Price Targets on Texas-Heavy Miners</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>KBW downgraded Cipher and CleanSpark to Market Perform on Wednesday and cut both price targets to $9.50, from $11 and $11.50. It lowered its odds of Cipher signing a Reveille lease in the next 12 months from 100% to 50%, and cut CleanSpark's Sealy and Brazoria phase 1 leases to 50% from 65% (Blockspace). Glagola wrote that regulatory uncertainty would "weigh on lease timing" across their "Texas-heavy pipelines."</li>
        <li>Bernstein cut targets on seven miners-turned-AI developers by roughly 35% to 54%, including IREN ($100 to $55), Core Scientific ($32 to $19), Riot ($35 to $18) and Cipher ($23 to $15). Analyst Gautam Chhugani said more than half the power capacity the group has or plans is in Texas, and that he expects the data center pause to continue (CoinDesk).</li>
        <li>Morgan Stanley said it would re-examine Cipher's Colchis site, Galaxy's Helios II project and MARA's expansion plans. It said Riot's existing grid connections at Corsicana and Rockdale could gain value, and that Hut 8 could benefit if developers move projects to Louisiana (CoinDesk).</li>
        <li>Goldman Sachs puts 25% of planned U.S. data center capacity in Texas, Reuters reported. CNN cited a Wells Fargo estimate that every 10% cut in planned Texas capacity would reduce Big Tech's AI infrastructure spending by 1.6%. Cooley's Mona Dajani told CNN that Texas law grandfathers some local permits, but that doesn't cover state permitting changes or electric service guarantees Hinojosa could vacate.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Analysts are now sorting Texas projects by one question: is the power already approved? Leases that are still being negotiated took the haircut, and existing interconnections, like Riot's, are being treated as more valuable, not less.</p>

      <p>No new TCEQ news releases since Oct. 21, and no new data center or gas plant air permit actions turned up in the eight tracked counties. No new zoning filings were reported in the DFW counties, and no new behind-the-meter generation deals were announced in Texas. ERCOT's news releases still stop at Sept. 21, and nobody has reported what Bastrop's council did Tuesday on the Cedar Creek gas plant.</p>

